es sense--why is 5 both a quantum computer (whcih iirc are extremely rare) and a millisecond accurate clock (which are common, and cheaper than the state of the art complexity 4 computer)? Plus, it mentions those swinging perpetual motion machines, but that's much less complex than a punch card reader or steam engine.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-09T16:35:02-07:00","id":129388,"title":"","content":"Complexity chart is more a quick guide, 1-5 obviously if you include decimals or even halfs you can reach closer to real world scale of complexity but that complicated the system almost unnessisarily","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2909-mr-f-i-x","name":"Mr.F.I.X.","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2909_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1053"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}